{
  "concept": "Bellman-Ford finds shortest paths by relaxing every edge once per pass, |V|−1 times, letting improvements — including those caused by negative-weight edges — propagate one hop further with each pass.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Five vertex circles (light gray fill, black outline) labeled outside with italic letters: s at far left, t upper center-left, y lower center-left, x upper right, z lower right",
    "Distance estimate d[v] rendered large inside each circle (starts as 0 for s and ∞ for t, x, y, z)",
    "Directed edge s→t labeled 6",
    "Directed edge s→y labeled 7",
    "Directed edge t→x labeled 5 (curved arc over the top)",
    "Directed edge x→t labeled −2 (curved arc returning below the 5-arc)",
    "Directed edge t→y labeled 8 (vertical, t down to y)",
    "Directed edge t→z labeled −4 (diagonal from t down-right to z)",
    "Directed edge y→x labeled −3 (diagonal from y up-right to x)",
    "Directed edge y→z labeled 9 (horizontal, y to z)",
    "Directed edge z→x labeled 7 (vertical, z up to x)",
    "Directed edge z→s labeled 2 (long diagonal from z back to s)",
    "Bold gray highlight stroke drawn underneath the edges currently in the predecessor subgraph (π-tree)",
    "Pass counter / caption text: 'Initialization' or 'Pass k of 4'",
    "Change counter: 'distances improved this pass: n'",
    "Edge-order list panel showing the fixed relaxation order: (t,x) (t,y) (t,z) (x,t) (y,x) (y,z) (z,x) (z,s) (s,t) (s,y)",
    "Current-edge marker: the edge under consideration flashes in a contrasting color with its tentative sum d[u]+w shown",
    "Final answer annotation for panel (e): d = {s:0, t:2, x:4, y:7, z:−2}"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "relaxEditor",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That the entire algorithm is one three-line test — 'is going through u cheaper?' — and that the order and number of times you apply it is what makes shortest paths converge; a wrong or missing comparison visibly stalls or corrupts the propagation.",
      "label": "Implement the relaxation step",
      "entry_point": "relax(u, v, w)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "d(v)", "does": "current shortest-path estimate of vertex v (Infinity if not yet reached)" },
        { "op": "setD(v, value)", "does": "write a new estimate into vertex v's circle — the ONLY way to change a distance" },
        { "op": "setPred(v, u)", "does": "record u as v's predecessor; the shaded gray tree is drawn from these links" },
        { "op": "w(u, v)", "does": "weight of edge u→v (also handed to you as the third argument)" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "attach your own caption to the current frame, e.g. note('improved x via y')" }
      ],
      "frame": "the whole 5-vertex graph with all five d-values, the edge (u,v) currently being tested drawn in the highlight color with its tentative sum d[u]+w, the shaded predecessor tree so far, the pass number, and the running count of improvements",
      "input": { "label": "Edge relaxation order (fixed, 4 passes)", "default": "(t,x,5) (t,y,8) (t,z,-4) (x,t,-2) (y,x,-3) (y,z,9) (z,x,7) (z,s,2) (s,t,6) (s,y,7)" },
      "sample_code": "// Called once per edge, ten edges per pass, four passes.\n// dist starts at s = 0, everything else Infinity.\nfunction relax(u, v, w) {\n  if (d(u) + w < d(v)) {\n    setD(v, d(u) + w);\n    setPred(v, u);\n    note('improved ' + v + ' via ' + u);\n  }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function relax(u, v, w) {\n  // BUG: compares the endpoints' estimates but forgets the edge weight,\n  // so it copies d(u) into v and ignores the cost of the hop.\n  if (d(u) < d(v)) {\n    setD(v, d(u));\n    setPred(v, u);\n  }\n}",
      "success_check": "After 4 passes every edge (u,v) satisfies d(v) <= d(u) + w(u,v), and the final estimates are s=0, t=2, x=4, y=7, z=-2 with the predecessor tree s→y→x→t→z."
    },
    {
      "id": "passStepper",
      "type": "state",
      "teaches": "That each pass extends correct shortest paths by exactly one more edge, so panels (a)–(e) are snapshots of the same code run five times over the same edge list.",
      "label": "Snapshot",
      "states": ["init", "pass1", "pass2", "pass3", "pass4"],
      "default": "init"
    },
    {
      "id": "showTree",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the shaded gray edges are not 'edges just relaxed' but the current predecessor subgraph — a tree that gets rewired whenever a better route is found.",
      "label": "Shade predecessor tree",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "(a) Initialization",
      "narration": "Every estimate starts at ∞ except the source s, which is 0. All ten edges are thin and unshaded — no predecessor links exist yet.",
      "state": { "passStepper": "init", "showTree": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "(b) After pass 1",
      "narration": "Only edges leaving s can improve anything on the first sweep: d[t] becomes 6 and d[y] becomes 7. Paths of length one edge are now correct.",
      "state": { "passStepper": "pass1", "showTree": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "(c) After pass 2",
      "narration": "With t and y settled, relaxing y→x (−3) gives d[x] = 4 and t→z (−4) gives d[z] = 2. Two-edge paths are now correct, and the tree rewires to s→y→x.",
      "state": { "passStepper": "pass2", "showTree": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "(d) After pass 3",
      "narration": "The negative edge x→t pays off: going s→y→x→t costs 2, beating the direct edge of weight 6. d[t] drops from 6 to 2 and t's parent switches from s to x.",
      "state": { "passStepper": "pass3", "showTree": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "(e) After pass 4 — converged",
      "narration": "The improved d[t] = 2 finally flows through t→z, pushing d[z] to −2. A fifth pass would change nothing: every edge now satisfies d[v] ≤ d[u] + w.",
      "state": { "passStepper": "pass4", "showTree": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Break the relaxation",
      "narration": "Load the buggy version that drops the edge weight from the comparison and replay: distances flatten toward 0 and the shaded tree never reaches z correctly, showing that the whole algorithm lives in that one inequality.",
      "state": { "passStepper": "pass4", "showTree": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "One dumb rule — 'if going through u is cheaper, take it' — applied blindly to every edge, |V|−1 times, is enough to find all shortest paths: each pass lets a correct answer travel exactly one more edge outward, which is why a negative edge discovered late can still fix a vertex on a later pass.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single graph drawn once, large and centered, in the classic CLRS layout: s at far left mid-height; t above-right of s and y below-right of s forming a triangle; x at upper right and z at lower right. Vertices are light-gray filled circles with thin black outlines, the letter name in italic just outside the circle and the current d-value in large type inside. Edges are thin black arrows with weights in plain text beside them; t↔x is drawn as two curved arcs (5 going right over the top, −2 returning just below it), t→z and y→x cross in the middle forming an X, z→s is a long shallow diagonal back to the source, t→y is vertical, y→z horizontal, z→x vertical. Edges currently in the predecessor subgraph get a thick soft-gray highlight stroke drawn behind the black arrow. The edge being tested during replay flashes in a saturated accent color (e.g. orange) with a small floating label 'd[u]+w = …' next to it; when a relaxation succeeds the target vertex's number animates from old to new value and the old parent edge's gray shading fades out as the new one fades in. Below the graph sit the pass caption ('Pass 3 of 4'), the improvements-this-pass counter, and the fixed edge-order list with the current edge marked. Final converged state should read 0, 2, 4, 7, −2 for s, t, x, y, z with the gray tree tracing s→y→x→t→z."
}

